---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="5.3 Lean Production and Quality Management">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/ibbm/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <a href="/ibbm/unit5/">Unit 5</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <span>5.3 Lean Production and Quality Management</span>
  </div>
  <div class="topic-hero">
    <span class="topic-number">5.3</span>
    <h1>Lean Production and Quality Management <span class="hl-badge">HL Only</span></h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Cutting waste, and the different ways a business makes sure what it sells is good enough.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabs">
    <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="switchTab(this,'notes')">Notes</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'practice')">Practice</button>
  </div>

  <!-- NOTES -->
  <div id="notes" class="tab-content active">

    <div class="learning-goals">
      <div class="learning-goals-title">Learning Goals</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Explain lean production and its methods: kaizen and JIT</li>
        <li>Explain cradle to cradle design</li>
        <li>Distinguish quality control from quality assurance</li>
        <li>Explain quality circles, benchmarking and TQM</li>
        <li>Evaluate the impact of lean production and TQM, and the importance of quality standards</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Lean production</h2>
      <p><strong>Lean production</strong> streamlines operations to cut waste and raise efficiency.</p>
      <div class="key-terms">
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Kaizen</div><div class="definition">Continuous improvement: small, frequent changes suggested by managers and employees.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Just-in-time (JIT)</div><div class="definition">Small, regular orders delivered just in time for use, so little or no stock is held. More in <a href="/ibbm/unit5/5-6/">5.6</a>.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Cradle to cradle (C2C)</div><div class="definition">Designing products so every material can be reused, recycled or safely returned to nature at the end of its life, so nothing becomes waste.</div></div>
      </div>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Benefit</th><th>Limitation</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>JIT</strong></td><td>Better cash flow and lower costs; staff take care (no spare stock); more space for production</td><td>A late delivery can halt production; less resilience; poor for seasonal demand; smaller orders may lose economies of scale</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div>
        <p>The cradle to cradle definition was written to fill a gap in the lesson slides.</p>
      </div>
      <p>See it in practice: <a href="https://global.toyota/en/company/vision-and-philosophy/production-system" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Toyota's production system</a>, where much of lean production started.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Why quality matters</h2>
      <p>Quality covers appearance and design, image and reputation, reliability, durability, fitness for purpose, safety, customer service and after-sales support.</p>
      <div class="content-card">
        <div class="content-card-title">Sam's pencil: who gets hurt by a bad product?</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div><strong>The maker</strong> (Thien Long): reputation damage, lost repeat customers, returns and refunds, pressure to invest in QC/QA, retailer relationships at risk.</div>
          <div><strong>The seller</strong> (Phuong Nam): reputation by association, complaint handling costs, customers doubting its other stock.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Managing quality</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What it is</th><th>Key features</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Quality control (QC)</strong></td><td>Checking output against a set standard, usually at the end, and rejecting or reworking what fails</td><td>Inspection after production, often by a separate inspector; may use sampling. Focus: <strong>detecting</strong> defects</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Quality assurance (QA)</strong></td><td>Procedures built into every stage of production, with responsibility held by the workers doing the job</td><td>Checks throughout; documented standards at each stage. Focus: <strong>preventing</strong> defects</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Quality circles</strong></td><td>Small groups of workers from the same area who meet regularly to find and solve quality problems</td><td>Uses workers' hands-on knowledge; recommends to management, doesn't decide; linked to kaizen</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Benchmarking</strong></td><td>Measuring products, processes or performance against a standard, usually the industry's best, to find gaps and set targets</td><td>Internal (between branches) or external (against competitors). Focus: closing the gap, not just comparing</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Total quality management (TQM)</strong></td><td>A company-wide philosophy: every employee in every function is responsible for quality, aiming for continuous improvement and zero defects</td><td>The umbrella that QA, QC and quality circles can sit under; needs teamwork, training and a cultural shift, so it can be slow</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Quality standards</strong></td><td>Formal certifications (such as ISO 9001) for organisations that meet agreed quality management requirements</td><td>Voluntary or required; signal a minimum, not "the best"; give reputation, access to markets and contracts, and better processes; vary between countries</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">QC vs QA in one line</div>
        <p>QC <em>inspects quality in</em> at the end. QA <em>builds quality in</em> at every stage.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Impact of lean production and TQM</h2>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="callout key-term"><div class="callout-title">Upside</div><p>Less waste and cost; higher quality and customer satisfaction.</p></div>
        <div class="callout warning"><div class="callout-title">Catch</div><p>Needs investment, real staff buy-in and cultural change. Can fail or stall if imposed top-down without worker engagement.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-title">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Lean = less waste, greater efficiency; methods are kaizen and JIT, with C2C design aiming for zero waste</li>
        <li>QC detects defects at the end; QA prevents them throughout</li>
        <li>Quality circles, benchmarking and TQM are ways to manage quality; TQM is the umbrella</li>
        <li>Standards like ISO 9001 signal a minimum, open markets and contracts</li>
        <li>All of it depends on staff buy-in</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div><!-- /notes -->

  <!-- PRACTICE -->
  <div id="practice" class="tab-content">

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 1: Venn diagram</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Draw a three-circle Venn diagram: kaizen, JIT, cradle to cradle. What is unique to each, shared by two, and shared by all three? Add an advantage and a disadvantage beside each circle.</div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">One thing all three share</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">They all cut waste: wasted effort and errors (kaizen), wasted stock and space (JIT), and wasted materials at the end of a product's life (C2C).</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 2: Toyota</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Read <a href="https://global.toyota/en/company/vision-and-philosophy/production-system" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Toyota's production system page</a>. Find one example each of kaizen, JIT and C2C. Toyota has kept this going for decades: does the page show you the <em>systems</em> that do that, or just what they do?</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 3: Apply it to your school</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">For each of kaizen, JIT and C2C: what's working at your school, what could improve, and what system would keep it going?</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 4: What went wrong?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Find a product that failed on quality (a recall, a faulty batch). Was it a QC problem, a QA problem, a TQM problem, or a design problem? What would you do to stop it happening again? Why does it matter which one it was?</div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Why it matters</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">The fix depends on the cause. A QC failure needs better inspection. A QA failure needs better procedures at each stage. A design failure means even perfect production would still make a bad product.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 5: Advantages and disadvantages</div>
      <p class="reveal-hint">Write your own first, then reveal.</p>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="sort-grid">
          <div class="sort-item">Quality control<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">+ Stops faulty goods reaching customers. − Waste is already made; inspection costs money</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Quality assurance<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">+ Fewer defects, less waste. − Paperwork and training costs</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Quality circles<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">+ Uses workers' knowledge, motivating. − Meeting time; ideas wasted if managers ignore them</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Benchmarking<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">+ Clear targets from the best. − Hard to get competitors' data; copying, not leading</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">TQM<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">+ Quality becomes everyone's job. − Slow, costly cultural change; fails without buy-in</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Quality standards<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">+ Reputation, contract and market access. − Cost and time to certify; only a minimum</span></button></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout remember" style="margin-top:1rem;">
          <div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div>
          <p>These suggested answers are pending teacher review.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 6: Exam question, Vantex Furniture</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Vantex Furniture is a mid-sized manufacturer of office chairs, exporting to several countries in Southeast Asia. Faced with rising complaints about faulty chairs and increasing competition from lower-cost rivals, the operations director is considering two changes: introducing regular quality circles on the factory floor, and applying for ISO 9001 certification. The finance director is concerned about the cost and time these changes would require, given the company's tight margins.</p>
        <div class="exercise-q">a) Explain what is meant by a quality circle. [2 marks]</div>
        <div class="exercise-q">b) Explain one way in which ISO 9001 certification could benefit Vantex Furniture. [2 marks]</div>
        <div class="exercise-q">c) Discuss the advantages and disadvantages, for Vantex Furniture, of introducing quality circles. [8 marks]</div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Points to use (pending teacher review)</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">
            a) A small group of workers from the same area who meet regularly to identify and solve quality problems, and recommend solutions to management.<br />
            b) It signals a recognised quality standard to overseas buyers, helping Vantex win back trust after the complaints and compete on more than price.<br />
            c) For: floor workers know where chairs go wrong, so faults are fixed at source; fewer complaints and returns; more motivated staff. Against: meetings take production time, a real cost with tight margins; results are slow; it only works if managers act on suggestions. Judge: does fixing faults save more than it costs, given the complaints are rising now?
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 7: Write the exam questions</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Write a 5.3 question for each command term: define, compare, distinguish, explain, identify, discuss, to what extent. Swap with a classmate and answer theirs.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /practice -->

  <div class="page-nav">
    <a href="/ibbm/unit5/5-2/" class="prev">
      <span class="nav-label">← Previous</span>
      <span class="nav-title">5.2 Operations Methods</span>
    </a>
    <a href="/ibbm/unit5/5-4/" class="next">
      <span class="nav-label">Next →</span>
      <span class="nav-title">5.4 Location</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</Layout>
